==Introduction==

===Abstract===

This document proposes an encoding format using codex32 for the key material that appears in a
[https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0380.mediawiki BIP-0380] descriptor <code>KEY</code> expression. Each
kind of key material gets its own human-readable part and its own serialization. It depends on the codex32 HRP
generalization, since BIP-0093 currently fixes the HRP as <code>ms</code>.

===Copyright===

This document is licensed under the 3-clause BSD license.

===Motivation===

A descriptor <code>KEY</code> expression carries a hex public key, a WIF private key, or a base58 extended key, any of
which may have to survive being copied by hand, printed, photographed, or read aloud. None of those formats has
codex32's guarantees and properties: a single mistyped character in an xpub or in its key origin information either
fails opaquely or silently resolves to different, valid key material. Base58's checksum detects errors but cannot locate
or correct them, and neither base58 nor hex can be split into shares.

The codex32 format supplies error detection, error correction and Shamir secret sharing for the same material, and,
because an uppercase codex32 string is drawn from QR's alphanumeric character set while base58 is not, it encodes an
extended key to a QR symbol one version smaller than base58 does, despite being longer as text.

==Specification==

===Serialization===

Each payload is the key material with every bit that the HRP already implies removed: the base58 version bytes, and the
<code>0x00</code> that BIP-0032 places in front of a private key's 32-byte scalar.

A compressed public key <code>serP(K)</code> is serialized in 257 bits wherever it appears, bare or inside an extended
key: one parity bit, 0 when <code>serP(K)</code> begins <code>0x02</code> and 1 when it begins <code>0x03</code>,
followed by the 256-bit x-coordinate most significant bit first.

Every payload is packed into 5-bit symbols most significant bit first, each symbol's most significant bit being the
earlier payload bit. Every payload is fixed length, so the spare bits left over in its final symbol are always present,
and their contents are specified rather than left as padding. These layouts describe the secret, share index
<code>s</code>, whether unshared or recovered. Every other share's payload is random, and implementations MUST NOT apply
this document's payload rules to it. Shares keep every bit of every symbol, spare bits included, since all of them
take part in interpolation.

The spare-bit CRC of width ''n'', for ''n'' from 1 to 4, is <code>M(x) * x^n mod G(x)</code> XORed with ''n'' one bits.
The coefficients of <code>M(x)</code> are a 1 followed by every payload bit preceding the spare bits, most significant
first. <code>G(x)</code> is the integer <code>(1 &lt;&lt; n) | 3</code>: <code>x^n + x + 1</code>, or
<code>x + 1</code> at width 1. At width 1 there is no final XOR, so the bit, called parity below, gives the payload an
odd number of one bits. Where the table below says CRC-''n'' or parity, the spare bits hold this CRC. Other codex32
applications MAY fill their spare bits the same way.

{| class="wikitable"
! HRP !! Key material !! Payload bits !! Symbols !! Spare bits !! Spare bits carry !! String length
|-
| <code>pk</code> || Public key, compressed || 257 || 52 || 3 || CRC-3 || 74
|-
| <code>pk</code> || Public key, uncompressed || 520 || 104 || 0 || — || 128
|-
| <code>xo</code> || X-only public key (BIP-0340) || 256 || 52 || 4 || CRC-4 || 74
|-
| <code>wf</code> || Private key, any network || 256 || 52 || 4 || network and compression status || 74
|-
| <code>xpub</code>/<code>tpub</code> || Extended public key || 590 || 118 || 0 || — || 144
|-
| <code>xprv</code>/<code>tprv</code> || Extended private key || 589 || 118 || 1 || parity || 144
|}

===Extended key fields===

Extended keys carry BIP-0032's fields in this order, which places the child number ahead of the parent fingerprint:

{| class="wikitable"
! Field !! Bits !! Characters !! Contents
|-
| Depth || 10 || 2 || Values 0 to 255
|-
| Child number || 35 || 7 || Hardened flag, then 3 zero bits, then the 31-bit index
|-
| Parent fingerprint || 32 || — || The 32-bit fingerprint, unpadded
|-
| Chain code || 256 || — || Begins at bit 77 in both the public and private forms
|-
| Key || 257 or 256 || — || <code>serP(K)</code> as 257 bits, or the scalar as 256
|}

An extended public key is 590 bits, exactly 118 symbols. An extended private key is 589 bits in the same 118 symbols,
with one spare bit at the end carrying a parity of the rest. Both are 144 characters.

The first nine characters are readable: two for depth and seven for the child number. Unhardened index 0
reads <code>qqqqqqq</code>, hardened index 0 reads <code>sqqqqqq</code>, and index 1 ends in <code>p</code> instead of
<code>q</code>.

===Key origin===

A key with BIP-0380 key origin information carries it in the HRP: the bracketed origin exactly as BIP-0380 writes it,
followed by the type (<code>pk</code>, <code>xo</code>, <code>wf</code>, <code>xpub</code>, <code>xprv</code>,
<code>tpub</code> or <code>tprv</code>), as in <code>[d34db33f/48h/0h/0h/2h]xpub1</code>. Without an origin the HRP is
the type alone. The checksum covers the whole HRP, so the origin is checksummed exactly as written, and every share of
a key carries its origin. Encoders SHOULD write hardened steps with <code>h</code>. Inside a descriptor the string MUST
be lower case, since BIP-0380 rejects an upper-case hardened marker. The HRP is at most 83 characters, as in BIP-0173.

===Trailing derivation steps===

Trailing derivation steps after an extended key MUST remain outside the string, in BIP-0380 syntax, and are applied
exactly once, as for a base58 extended key. The 3 zero bits in the child number MUST be zero when encoding, and decoders
MUST reject a nonzero value or a depth above 255.

===Private keys===

<code>wf</code> is a single HRP for every network. Network and compression status occupy the payload's 4 spare bits,
placed in the first payload symbol: the status in positions 4 to 1, and the scalar's most significant bit in position 0.
The scalar's remaining 255 bits fill the other 51 symbols, so each status has two possible first characters, differing
only in that scalar bit.

{| class="wikitable"
! Status !! Status bits !! First character !! WIF's leading character
|-
| mainnet, uncompressed || <code>0111</code> || <code>w</code> or <code>0</code> || <code>5</code>
|-
| mainnet, compressed || <code>1011</code> || <code>k</code> or <code>h</code> || '''<code>K</code>''' or <code>L</code>
|-
| testnet, uncompressed || <code>0000</code> || <code>q</code> or <code>p</code> || <code>9</code>
|-
| testnet, compressed || <code>1100</code> || <code>c</code> or <code>e</code> || '''<code>c</code>'''
|}

Only these 8 of the 32 possible first symbols are valid. Every mainnet character is at least 3 bits from every testnet
character, and the two statuses within a network are 2 bits apart.

===Error correction===

Implementations MAY correct errors anywhere in the codeword, the expanded HRP included, where a substituted HRP
character changes one or two symbols. They MUST NOT exclude the type or any other position from the search, and a
corrected origin MUST be a valid BIP-0380 origin. Implementations SHOULD NOT apply a correction that changes the type or
the status character of a <code>wf</code> secret, and SHOULD report it instead.

===Identifier===

No default identifier may be derived from the payload, for any profile in this document. Identifiers SHOULD be chosen as
BIP-0093 already suggests, from something the holder can reconstruct that is not the key. For a multisignature wallet,
each cosigner's key SHOULD carry its own identifier naming ''that cosigner'', and implementations SHOULD NOT give every
key in a set the same identifier.

===Recovery===

A recovered secret that decodes as a valid key is not proof that the shares came from one set. Before using a
recovered key, implementations SHOULD check it against something stored apart from the shares, such as its master
fingerprint, a descriptor, or a known address.

===Generation from fresh shares===

Splitting a key that already exists works for every profile: the secret is encoded first, with its CRC or status bits
set, and shares are then generated as in BIP-0093.

BIP-0093's procedure for generating a fresh secret from freely chosen shares applies only where the payload is itself
the secret. Public-key profiles MUST be derived from a private key that already exists, and MUST NOT be generated this
way.

{| class="wikitable"
! Profile !! Freely generatable? !! Constraint on a drawn payload !! Expected draws
|-
| <code>wf</code> || Yes || 4 status bits must match the chosen status || 16
|-
| <code>xprv</code>/<code>tprv</code> || In principle || Depth, parent fingerprint and child number zero, parity matching || about 2<sup>78</sup>
|-
| <code>pk</code>, <code>xo</code>, <code>xpub</code>, <code>tpub</code> || No || Result has no known private key || —
|}

The status is chosen first, and the last share is redrawn until the recovered secret carries it. A fresh master key
comes from a fresh <code>ms</code> seed, so <code>xprv</code> is for keys that already exist.

==Rationale==

'''Payloads.''' The HRP says whether a key is public or private, so the version bytes and BIP-0032's <code>0x00</code>
carry no information. With a compressed key's prefix as one bit, a compressed <code>pk</code>, an <code>xo</code> and a
<code>wf</code> are all 74 characters, like <code>cl</code> and a 256-bit <code>ms</code> seed, so keys interchangeable
in a script are interchangeable in shape.

'''CRCs.''' BIP-0173 places commonly confused characters one bit apart (<code>2</code>/<code>z</code>,
<code>5</code>/<code>s</code>, <code>m</code>/<code>n</code> and others), and these CRCs detect every single-bit error:
over random payloads, CRC-3 catches 90.3% of all substitutions against 87.5% by chance, and CRC-4 96.8% against 93.8%.
<code>x^n + x + 1</code> is primitive at widths 2 to 4, so its period of 2<sup>''n''</sup> - 1 is the longest possible,
and covering every bit before the spare bits is free, since the flipped bits of one character are at most 4 apart. The
leading 1, which bech32 and codex32 checksums also start from, makes zero bits added in front of the covered bits change
the CRC. The final XOR does the same for zeros added after the spare bits, which would otherwise pass whenever a longer
length of the same HRP has the same width, as 192-bit and 512-bit <code>ms</code> seeds do. Either insertion passes only
if the number of added bits is a multiple of the period. Any nonzero final XOR catches the second, so the all-zero
payload chooses among them: it passes exactly when the final XOR equals <code>x^(5k) mod G(x)</code> for its ''k''
symbols. At width 4 those values are only <code>1</code>, <code>x^2 + x</code> and <code>x^2 + x + 1</code>, so all ones
fails every all-zero payload. At widths 2 and 3 every nonzero value passes one length in 3 or 7, and all ones passes
none of the lengths used by <code>ms</code>, <code>cl</code> or this document. A parity bit can detect neither
insertion, so it takes no final XOR, which keeps every all-zero payload failing. Because the CRC-valid payloads are not
closed under GF(32) interpolation, a CRC checks the recovered secret rather than each share, so it catches most
recoveries from wrong or mixed shares, which the checksum cannot. A random recovered payload still decodes as a valid
key about 1 time in 4 for <code>wf</code>, 1 in 16 for a compressed <code>pk</code>, 1 in 32 for <code>xo</code> and 1
in 64 for an extended key.

'''Extended key layout.''' Putting the child number before the fingerprint aligns both readable fields. Depth takes two
characters so that every BIP-0032 depth is representable; one character would cap it at 31 and save only the 144th
character, which costs no QR version. The flag-padding-index split makes the hardened flag read
<code>s</code>, where right-aligning gives <code>z</code> and left-aligning turns index 1 into <code>g</code>. The
zero bits are fixed rather than a CRC because a CRC would mix into the characters that make the child number readable,
and the chain code and fingerprint are left unaligned because aligning noise buys nothing.

'''Status codes.''' Network confusion is unrecoverable and compression confusion is not, so the codes maximise distance
between networks; 3 is the most four 4-bit codes allow. Reaching a third WIF character, or WIF's
<code>K</code>/<code>L</code> pair by duplicating the scalar bit, would put the two networks one bit apart. WIF's
leading character is a side effect of base58's numeric range, while here the status is a specified field and each
network has its own characters. A dedicated 53rd status symbol would match WIF's characters exactly but break the
74-character shape, so it is left as an open question.

'''HRPs.''' Every pair of Bitcoin HRPs in use (<code>bc</code>, <code>tb</code>, <code>ms</code>, <code>cl</code>,
<code>ln</code>, <code>sp</code>) differs in both characters, and every HRP here keeps that rule against them and each
other. That leaves no name beginning "private" or "secret" or ending "key". Even so, <code>wf</code> and
<code>pk</code> are two expanded symbols apart, as are <code>xprv</code> and <code>xpub</code>, so a correction that
changes the type is reported rather than applied: it could turn a private key into a public one. The search still
covers the type. Excluding it gives up corrections the checksum guarantees, and a decoder that then searches past that
guarantee to explain an error in the type with errors elsewhere can return a false correction.

* <code>pk</code> is how descriptors already spell "public key", in <code>pk()</code>, <code>pkh()</code> and
  <code>wpkh()</code>. Public keys carry no network, as in BIP-0380, and a <code>tpk</code>-shaped HRP is registered in
  SLIP-0173. Uncompressed keys stay because BIP-0380 permits them and paper wallets hold them.
* <code>wf</code> is WIF without the excluded <code>i</code>. <code>se</code>, <code>sv</code> and <code>sd</code> are
  one from <code>sp</code>, and <code>pr</code>, <code>pv</code> and <code>sk</code> are one from <code>pk</code>. A
  <code>twif</code> would share the shape of <code>tpub</code> while holding something else.
* [https://lists.linuxfoundation.org/pipermail/bitcoin-dev/2018-May/016038.html Bech32X] (2018, never implemented) used
  <code>pk</code> for a mainnet ''private'' key, which is why the private-key HRP is kept far from it.
  [[bip-0178.mediawiki|BIP-0178]] records a key's script type and stays in base58.

'''Key origins.''' The origin goes in the HRP so that the checksum and error correction cover it as well as the key. A
descriptor parser already reads the bracketed origin in front of a key, so the string parses unchanged, and only the
checksum is computed over a longer HRP. Writing <code>h</code> leaves the brackets as the only characters of an
upper-case HRP outside QR's alphanumeric set.

'''Identifiers.''' An identifier computed from the key can be recomputed by anyone who later learns it. The BIP-0032
fingerprint links a share to P2PKH and P2WPKH outputs for <code>pk</code> and <code>wf</code>, to shared key origin
information for extended keys, and to a Taproot output for <code>xo</code> once a script path
reveals the internal key.

'''Fresh public keys.''' Interpolating random shares to a public key yields a point whose discrete logarithm nobody
knows, so funds paid to it are unspendable.

'''Root nodes.''' A BIP-0032 root node is an <code>xprv</code> at depth zero, so it gets no HRP of its own, which would
be a second encoding of the same key. Shorter root-only codex32 formats, such as Coinkite's CX1, remain available to
those who want one.

'''QR size.''' An uppercase codex32 string is entirely within QR's alphanumeric character set, at 5.5 bits per character
against 8 in byte mode. Measured with a QR encoder at error-correction level L:

{| class="wikitable"
! Key material !! This document !! QR version !! Existing format !! QR version
|-
| Extended key || 144 characters || 5 || 111 base58 characters || 6
|-
| Public key, compressed || 74 characters || 3 || 66 hex characters || 4 lowercase, 3 uppercase
|-
| X-only public key || 74 characters || 3 || 64 hex characters || 4 lowercase, 3 uppercase
|-
| Public key, uncompressed || 128 characters || 5 || 130 hex characters || 6 lowercase, 5 uppercase
|-
| Private key || 74 characters || 3 || 52 WIF characters || 3
|}

Base58 is mixed case and can never use alphanumeric mode, so the extended-key saving is unconditional. The hex saving
holds only against hex written in lower case, as descriptors write it. The private key ties with WIF at every
error-correction level and no arrangement can beat it: the 52-symbol scalar and the 13-character checksum alone already
exceed the next smaller version at L, M and H, and leave too little room for any HRP and header at Q.

===What the human-readable part discloses===

The HRP names the kind of material it protects, so a <code>wf</code> or <code>xprv</code> string announces a private key
and a <code>pk</code> or <code>xo</code> string a public one. This is inherent: a decoder needs to know which payload it
holds, and secret sharing operates on the data part, so the HRP is the same in every share of a set. Holders who need
the kind of material concealed must conceal the strings themselves. An origin also discloses the master fingerprint and
derivation path, in every share.

==Versioning==

There is no version field. The type is the version: an incompatible change to any payload layout takes a new type, and
a decoder that does not recognise a type declines to decode rather than guessing. Implementations MUST NOT attempt to
decode an unrecognised type under a known layout.

==QR encoding==

Encoders SHOULD upper-case a codex32 string before encoding it, since a lower-case string forces byte mode and forfeits
the alphanumeric saving.

The brackets of an origin are not in QR's alphanumeric set, so encoders SHOULD put each bracket in a byte-mode segment
and the rest of the upper-cased string in alphanumeric segments, rather than encode the whole string in byte mode. For a
144-character extended key with a four-step origin this is 974 bits against 1348, version 6 rather than 8 at level L.
Segmentation is ordinary QR, so nothing is required of decoders, which lower-case the string as BIP-0093 requires.

==Backwards compatibility==

These are new HRPs under the generalized-HRP codex32 grammar. Nothing changes for existing <code>ms</code> or
<code>cl</code> strings, for BIP-0380 descriptors, or for WIF, hex and base58 serializations, which remain valid inputs
wherever they are accepted today.

==Open questions==

* Whether to spend a 53rd symbol on a dedicated status character, buying exact parity with WIF's leading characters and
  a CRC-4 at the cost of the shape match with <code>pk</code>.
* Whether to pad the fingerprint as well, at 145 characters, to free 2 bits for a CRC over the extended key metadata.
* Whether the extended-key HRPs should stay 4 characters, given that <code>xprv</code> and <code>tprv</code> are then
  told apart by content rather than by shape.

==Reference implementation==

An experimental Python key-material codec and interoperability checks are provided in
<code>bip-codex32-key-expressions/</code>. It imports the generic codex32 framing and checksum implementation from
<code>python-codex32</code>. The layouts, key origins and key validation in that directory are independent code. It
does not parse descriptors.

==Test vectors==

To be written once the payload layouts stop moving. They must cover, for each profile: the bit packing of a known key,
the CRC or parity value, the <code>wf</code> status characters for all four network and compression combinations, an
extended key at depth zero and one at depth greater than zero with a hardened child number, a key with an origin,
trailing derivation steps outside the string, and invalid cases for a wrong CRC, nonzero child-number padding and an
unrecognised type.
